===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|+}}

# {{lb|en|Islam}} [[almsgiving|Almsgiving]], one of the [[five pillars]] of [[Islam]], in the [[form#Noun|form]] of an [[annual#Adjective|annual]] [[tax#Noun|tax]] on certain [[type#Noun|types]] of [[property#Noun|property]] which is then [[use#Verb|used]] for [[charitable]] [[purpose#Noun|purposes]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=&#91;{{w|Burhan al-Din al-Marghinani}}&#93;|chapter=I|translator=[[w:Charles Hamilton (orientalist)|Charles Hamilton]]|title=[[w:Al-Hidayah|The Hedàya, or Guide; a Commentary on the Mussulman Laws:{{nb...|Translated by Order of the Governor-General and Council of Bengal, by Charles Hamilton.}}]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by}} [[w:Thomas Bensley|T[homas] Bensley]]|year=1791|volume=I|section=book I (Of Zakât)|page=2|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Pa9CAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA2|oclc=265001673|passage='''{{smallcaps|Zakât}}''' is an ordinance of God, incumbent upon every perſon vvho is free, ſane, adult, and a Muſſulman, provided he be poſſeſſed, in full propriety, of ſuch eſtate or effects as are termed in the language of the lavv a ''Niſâb'', and that he has been in poſſeſſion of the ſame for the ſpace of ''one complete year'', vvhich is denominated ''Havvlân-Hâvvl''. The reaſon of this obligation is found in the vvord of God, vvho has ordained it in the {{smallcaps|Koran}}, ſaying, "{{smallcaps|Bestovv '''Zakât'''}}."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=James Butler Bryan|chapter=XII|title=A Practical View of Ireland, from the Period of the Union; with Plans for the Permanent Relief of Her Poor, and the Improvement of Her Municipal Organization:{{nb...|To which is Annexed, a Comparative Survey of the Laws and Institutions of Foreign States, for the Maintenance, Education, and Protection of the Working Classes.}}|location=Dublin|publisher=W. F. Wakeman,{{nb...|9, D’Olier-Street}}; London: [[w:Simpkin & Marshall|Simpkin and Marshall]],{{nb...|Stationers’-Court, London.}}|year=1831|page=327|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=LINIAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA327|oclc=966378098|passage=The Mahometan law insists on the legal obligation of society to support their indigent fellow-citizens, the Hedaya or Mahometan Guide begins, "take '''zakat'''," said [[w:Muhammad|Mahomet]], "from the rich Mussulmans, and bestow it upon the poor Mussulmans: '''zakat''' or charity," says the code, "signifies alms ''imposed by law'', in opposition to [sadka], voluntary charity." {{...}} The '''zakat''' was a property-tax paid on all productions and profits, on merchandise, mines, &c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:William A. Phillips|William A[ddison] Phillips]]|chapter=The Mahometan System, and the Governments and Forms of Society Founded on It|title=Labor, Land and Law: A Search for the Missing Wealth of the Working Poor|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Charles Scribner's Sons|Charles Scribner’s Sons]]|year=1886|page=140|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=06k3AAAAMAAJ&pg=PA140|oclc=8851153|passage=The Alcoran and all the subsequent teachings of the faithful enjoined a constant and liberal charity. {{...}} The theory of the '''zakat''', or tax, is that it is a voluntary gift. Contributing for the needs of the state followed in due course of time, and became obligatory. These taxes, or '''zakat''', amounted by law to two and a half per cent. of the principal of certain assessed property.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Ibn Battuta|chapter=Syria|translator=[[w:H. A. R. Gibb|H[amilton] A[lexander] R[osskeen] Gibb]]|title=[[w:The Rihla|The Travels of Ibn Baṭṭūṭa A.D. 1325–1354{{nb...|Translated with Revisions and Notes from the Arabic Text Edited by C[harles] Defrémery and B[eniamino] R[affaello] Sanguinetti by H. A. R. Gibb}}]]|location=Cambridge, Cambridgeshire|publisher=[[w:Cambridge University Press|University Press]] for the {{w|Hakluyt Society}}|year=1958|volume=I|page=72|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/workss2v110hakl/page/72/mode/1up|oclc=1014788231|passage=Amongst these stations is the well-known place called Qaṭyā, which the people pronounce Qaṭya, where '''''zakāt''''' is collected from the merchants, their goods are examined, and their baggage most rigorously searched.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:E. L. Peters|Emrys L[loyd] Peters]]|chapter=Aspects of Rank and Status among Muslims in a Lebanese Village|editor=w:Julian Pitt-Rivers|title=Mediterranean Countrymen: Essays in Social Anthropology of the Mediterranean|location=Paris|publisher=[[w:De Gruyter|Mouton and Co.]]|year=1963|page=170|oclc=439177938|newversion=republished in|editor2=Louise E. Sweet|title2=Peoples and Cultures of the Middle East: An Anthropological Reader|volume2=II (Life in the Cities, Towns, and Countryside)|location2=Garden City, N.Y.|publisher2=Natural History Press for the {{w|American Museum of Natural History}}|year2=1970|page2=88|pageurl2=https://archive.org/details/peoplescultureso0002loui/page/88/mode/1up|isbn2=978-0-385-00378-0|passage=Services given by the Sayyids are supposed to be free, but they should receive the '''''Zikat''''', or one tenth of the fruits of the earth.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Seymour Becker|chapter=Economic Development|title=Russia’s Protectorates in Central Asia: Bukhara and Khiva, 1865–1924|location=London; New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Routledge|RoutledgeCurzon]]|year=2004|year_published=2005|section=part 3 (The Russian Presence)|page=136|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=m9iHogrz3N4C&pg=PA136|isbn=978-0-415-32803-6|passage={{...}} Russian goods were subject to numerous '''zakats''' as well as to taxes on buying and selling in the bazaars, on maintaining shops and stalls in the bazaars, and on the compulsory use of the official bazaar weights.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Timur Kuran|chapter=Islam and Economics: Policy Prescriptions for a Free Society|editors=Sohrab Behdad; Farhad Nomani|title=Islam and the Everyday World: Public Policy Dilemmas|location=Abingdon, Oxfordshire; New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Routledge|year=2006|page=43|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=zVdlj3xa-7AC&pg=PA43|isbn=978-0-415-36823-0|passage=By tradition, '''zakat''' proceeds serve partly to deliver poor relief and partly to finance "praiseworthy activities," defined to include religious education and pilgrimage. In several countries, including Pakistan, '''zakat''' has become a legal obligation, and the government now organizes both its collection and its disbursement. In most parts of the Islamic world, however, '''zakat''' is collected and disbursed in a decentralized manner by local religious organizations{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Atta El-Battahani|chapter=Ideological Expansionist Movements versus Historical Indigenous Rights in the Darfur Region of Sudan: From Actual Homicide to Potential Genocide|editors=Salah M. Hassan; Carina E. Ray|title=Darfur and the Crisis of Governance in Sudan: A Critical Reader|location=Ithaca, N.Y.; London|publisher=w:Cornell University Press|year=2009|section=part 1, section 1 (Origins and Evolution of the Conflict), endnote 16|page=67|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_W0eUqHb4g4C&pg=PA67|isbn=978-0-8014-7594-8|passage='''''Zakat''''' is a system of alms giving which constitutes one of the five pillars of Islam, and '''zakat''' committees operate as social welfare agencies by collecting '''zakats''' from able Muslims and distributing them to the less fortunate.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Thomas Piketty|chapter=Ternary Societies and Colonialism: Eurasian Trajectories|translator=w:Arthur Goldhammer|title=w:Capital and Ideology|location=Cambridge, Mass.|publisher=The Belknap Press of {{w|Harvard University Press}}|month=March|year=2020|section=part 2 (Slave and Colonial Societies), footnote 76|page=409|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=7ybMDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA409|isbn=978-0-674-98082-2|passage=In practice, the '''zakats''' seem to have varied a great deal with the context, the society, and local norms.}}

